#====================================================================
# E232 — Swift Linux aarch64 toolchain tarball cannot run directly on OHOS
#====================================================================

# Layer 0: Error Surface
id:                     E232
trigger_tool:           swift|swiftc|swift-driver|swift-package|conan
phase:                  build|toolchain-probe
error_signature:        Swift 6 toolchain not found on PATH|swift-driver: Permission denied|swift-driver: No such file or directory|/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1|failed to map segment from shared object

# Layer 1: Recognition
severity:               2
category:               toolchain|swift|platform-limitation|linux-glibc
match_keywords:         Swift, swift-tools-version, SwiftPM, swift-driver, swiftc, ld-linux-aarch64.so.1, libc.so.6, libswiftCore.so, Foundation, OHOS
title:                  Swift Linux aarch64 toolchain tarball cannot run directly on OHOS

# Layer 2: Diagnosis
root_cause:             Official Swift Linux/aarch64 toolchain binaries are built for a Linux/glibc userspace. On OHOS they first hit executable policy/signing restrictions; after signing, direct execution fails because the requested interpreter /lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 is absent. A minimal Ubuntu glibc runtime bundle can make signed ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 start, but loader-mediated execution still fails with failed to map segment from shared object even for a tiny Ubuntu true binary, so user-space glibc bundling is insufficient on the tested environment. A follow-up check found no explicit noexec flag on /storage/Users/currentUser, and signed Swiftly plus signed ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 can start from the same probe area, so this is not a simple directory noexec issue.
affected_scope:         SwiftPM/Swift packages requiring a host Swift 6 toolchain on HarmonyOS PC, including unxip/3.3 and future packages with Package.swift swift-tools-version 6.x.
verification_cmd:       command -v swift || true; llvm-readobj --program-headers --dynamic-table usr/bin/swift-driver; binary-sign-tool sign -inFile swift-driver -outFile swift-driver.signed -selfSign 1; ./swift-driver --version

# Layer 3: Solution Space
rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           split-toolchain-prerequisite
rec_description:        Do not mix Swift toolchain migration into an application/library recipe. Mark the Swift package blocked, record dependency publication separately, and create a dedicated Swift toolchain adaptation task before retrying the package build.
rec_command:            CI_PACKAGES="archives/u/unxip/3.3" zsh ci/conan/build_and_test.sh
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               conan-recipe-blocker-record

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      probe-official-linux-toolchain
fallback_description:   Download the official Swift Linux/aarch64 tarball, record checksum, handle OHOS link extraction restrictions, sign a minimal executable, inspect ELF interpreter/NEEDED entries, and verify any proposed Linux/glibc compatibility route with a minimal glibc executable before retrying Swift.
fallback_command:       sh probe-swift-6.0.3.sh
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          manual-toolchain-probe
alt_probe:              Official Swiftly Linux/aarch64 installer is a static AArch64 ELF with no PT_INTERP/no dynamic section in local readelf inspection. On device, unsigned Swiftly fails with Permission denied; after signing and keeping the executable name swiftly, --help works. Stock init --skip-install then fails on missing system ca-certificates. A probe-only same-length CA bundle path patch plus re-signing lets init --platform ubuntu22.04 --skip-install succeed and list Swift 6.0.3 as available. A later install probe downloaded the full 735.9 MiB Swift 6.0.3 toolchain but failed extracting a link-style config entry aarch64-swift-linux-musl-clang++.cfg. Manual link-flattening of swift/swiftc/clang/cfg entries allowed direct ELF inspection, but signed swift and swift-frontend still fail with No such file or directory because they request /lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 and glibc runtime libraries. A minimal Ubuntu glibc runtime bundle made signed ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 runnable, but loader-mediated execution of both Swift and a tiny coreutils true binary failed with failed to map segment from shared object, so user-space glibc bundling is insufficient on the tested OHOS environment. Mount/execution check makes a simple noexec-directory explanation unlikely.
alt_probe_source:       https://download.swift.org/swiftly/linux/swiftly-aarch64.tar.gz
alt_probe_sha256:       cc4f912fff6c7f53704fc6d22f9e8ee7fdf6bd574ad276998f7502418bf5a45a
mainline_probe:         OHOS-native C/C++ substrate is usable on the true device: clang-15 reports aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos, sh ./clang and sh ./clang++ work when TMPDIR points to a writable user directory, and C/C++ smoke binaries build and run. A 2026-07-28 Swift 6.0.3 source-build probe progressed through IRGen memory-effects, TextAPI/TBD, and LLVM CAS shim compatibility gaps. The latest synced CAS fixes covered CASID errors, ObjectProxy data conversion, AsyncProxyValue::take, and CASID stream printing; the rebuild passed prior CachingUtils.cpp and CompileJobCacheResult.cpp failures, relinked libswiftAST.a/libswiftSIL.a, reached [270/807] in lib/Sema, then later resumes linked libswiftSema.a, entered SILOptimizer, entered IRGen, validated CompilerInvocation.cpp CAS/optional fixes, added the missing Clang serialized-diagnostics RECORD_SOURCE_FILE_CONTENTS record, compiled SerializedDiagnosticConsumer.cpp, and linked libswiftIRGen.a. A 2026-07-29 follow-up produced OHOS-runnable swift, swiftc, and swift-frontend binaries; swift --version reports Swift 6.0.3 target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos. Diagnostics narrowed the prior SwiftShims trap to ObjectFilePCHContainerGenerator reaching clang::EmitBackendOutput(... Backend_EmitObj ...) after PCHGenerator::Writer.WriteAST completed. A Swift ClangImporter experiment selecting raw PCM containers on OHOS and skipping ObjectFilePCHContainerWriter/Reader rebuilt successfully; clean-cache import SwiftShims passed with RC=0, and swiftCore-swiftmodule-ohos-aarch64 produced the OHOS/aarch64 Swift.swiftmodule and Swift.swiftdoc with RC=0. A 2026-07-30 follow-up completed swiftCore-ohos-aarch64: direct Swift.o emission crashed in LLVM AArch64 Assembly Printer, but an OHOS IR handoff emitted Swift.ll and compiled it through the OHOS LLVM fork's clang-15 into a valid Swift.o; after adding -fPIC to 95 OHOS/aarch64 runtime/support compile edges in generated build.ninja, lib/swift/ohos/aarch64/libswiftCore.so linked successfully as a 34 MiB aarch64 ELF shared object. The 2026-07-30 final constrained resume then completed stdlib/swift-stdlib-ohos-aarch64 with rc=0, producing OHOS/aarch64 Swift/Glibc/private/unittest swiftmodules and stdlib/runtime shared libraries. Remaining mainline work is source-level replacement for the temporary build-tree swiftc wrapper, IR-to-object handoff, PIC propagation, raw PCM and OHOS overlay experiments, then repeatable packaging and SwiftPM validation.

mainline_probe_swiftglibc: OHOS Platform/Glibc overlay generation is the next broad-stdlib boundary. A source experiment added OHOS to swiftGlibc target SDKs and glibc.modulemap generation, excluded OHOS from link "dl", guarded LibcOverlayShims semaphore wrappers with __has_include(<semaphore.h>), and reconfigured SWIFT_SDK_OHOS_ARCH_aarch64_PATH to /storage/Users/currentUser/usr/local/sysroot. The sysroot rerun build-swiftGlibc-ohos-aarch64-sysroot-20260730-122806.log reached at least [471/916] without a compile failure, then the true device entered sustained Out of memory/can't fork pressure. Treat this as a resource boundary until final remote state is confirmed.
mainline_probe_swiftglibc_evening: 2026-07-30 evening resume fixed SwiftGlibc module lookup by explicitly passing the generated OHOS glibc.modulemap, resolving assert.h through the staged OHOS sysroot, removing unavailable CUUID/uuid.h from the generated modulemap, and avoiding glibc-private __sighandler_t for OHOS. build-swiftGlibc-swiftmodule-ohos-aarch64-flags-20260730-184945.log completed with RC=0. The broader stdlib/swift-stdlib-ohos-aarch64 run then resumed with nice -n 10 ninja -j1; Swift.o and SwiftOnoneSupport.o use temporary IR-to-OHOS-clang object handoff, and generated OHOS/aarch64 C/C++ FLAGS were patched with -fPIC after libswiftCore.so exposed non-PIC runtime objects. As of 2026-07-30 19:25 CST build-stdlib-ohos-aarch64-full-j1-20260730-191240.log was at [132/1086] with no new failure marker and about 14 GiB MemAvailable.
mainline_probe_stdlib_private_late_evening: 2026-07-30 late evening resume advanced private stdlib targets after SwiftGlibc. SwiftPrivate.o and SwiftPrivateLibcExtras.o direct object emission trapped, but corrected -emit-ir produced non-empty IR; opt -verify, opt -S -strip-debug, and the OHOS clang wrapper produced valid aarch64 relocatable objects, then single Ninja edges copied those objects. SwiftPrivateThreadExtras.swift exposed an OHOS pthread_t import mismatch: OHOS imports pthread_t as an opaque pointer/optional shape, so the Linux pthread_t() initializer path is invalid; the remote source experiment excludes SWIFT_STDLIB_OS_OHOS from that branch and generated OHOS Swift commands now pass -D SWIFT_STDLIB_OS_OHOS. After that, SwiftPrivateThreadExtras.o reached the same object-emission trap; a watchdog-wrapped IR probe produced object-ready SwiftPrivateThreadExtras.strip-clang.o, and the single copy edge was installed after SSH recovered. The next full stdlib resume should start from the following graph node.
mainline_probe_stdlib_final: 2026-07-30 final constrained resume completed /storage/Users/currentUser/swift-mainline-ohos-src/logs/build-stdlib-ohos-aarch64-full-j1-20260730-230325.log with END=2026-07-30 23:04:01 CST rc=0. build.ninja contains -fmodule-map-file=/storage/Users/currentUser/swift-mainline-ohos-src/build/swift-manual-ohos-linuxsys2/lib/swift/ohos/aarch64/glibc.modulemap, a minimal import Glibc typecheck returned typecheck_rc=0, and swift/swiftc --version report Swift 6.0.3 target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos. Produced OHOS/aarch64 artifacts include libswiftCore.so, libswiftGlibc.so, libswiftSwiftOnoneSupport.so, libswiftSwiftPrivate.so, libswiftSwiftPrivateLibcExtras.so, libswiftSwiftPrivateThreadExtras.so, libswift_Builtin_float.so, libswiftRuntimeUnittest.so, libswiftStdlibUnittest.so, libswiftStdlibUnicodeUnittest.so, libswiftStdlibCollectionUnittest.so, libswiftRemoteMirror.so, Swift/Glibc/private/unittest swiftmodules, and bin/swift-reflection-test-OHOS. Spot checks show libswiftCore.so needs libc++_shared.so/libc.so, and libswiftGlibc.so needs libswiftSwiftOnoneSupport.so/libswiftCore.so/libc++_shared.so/libc.so. Follow-up user-program probes proved runtime viability through IR handoff: probe-hello-ir-continue-20260730-233205.log prints hello-ohos-swift with run_rc=0, and probe-hello-glibc-ir-run-20260730-234506.log prints hello-ohos-glibc with run_rc=0 after autolinking SwiftOnoneSupport/Core/Glibc. The 2026-07-31 frontend instrumentation narrowed the signal 5 root cause to GeneratedModule destruction of LLVM TargetMachine on OHOS. Releasing that TargetMachine in an experimental OHOS-host workaround made minimal emit-irgen rc=0, emit-ir rc=0, frontend -c rc=0 with an arm64 ELF relocatable object, and import Glibc emit-ir rc=0. Remaining boundaries are removal/formalization of probe logging and workaround, legacy driver link selection (currently clang -fuse-ld=gold instead of the proven OHOS wrapper/lld path), missing swift-driver-new, absent SwiftPM subcommands swift-package/swift-build, and unusable LLDB due Python 3.11 relocation symbols.

mainline_probe_driver_link: 2026-07-31 follow-up cleaned FrontendTool.cpp probe prints and rebuilt the OHOS TargetMachine lifetime workaround in build-swift-frontend-clean-targetmachine-release-20260731-005549.log with rc=0. Minimal swiftc -frontend emit-ir, frontend -c, and import Glibc emit-ir probes in probe-clean-frontend-20260731-010143 all returned rc=0. The full legacy driver was split into frontend, swift-autolink-extract, and link stages: probe-driver-segments-20260731-010632 showed frontend rc=0 and autolink rc=0, with link rc=126 because Swift directly execed the /storage/Users/currentUser/usr/local/bin/clang shell wrapper. Direct clang-15 ELF linking with lld and -Wl,--code-sign produced a runnable signed Swift binary. A small OHOS driver experiment changed GenericUnix default linker to lld for isOHOSFamily(), added -Wl,--code-sign to OHOS dynamic links, and selected executable clang-15 as the linker driver. Rebuild build-swift-frontend-ohos-driver-link-20260731-011056.log returned RC=0. probe-driver-after-ohos-link-20260731-011242 then showed the driver dry-run using /storage/Users/currentUser/usr/local/bin/clang-15 -fuse-ld=lld -pie -Wl,--code-sign, swiftc RUN_RC=0, and the produced program printed hello after driver patch with EXE_RC=0. Remaining package blockers are swift-driver-new absence, swift-package/swift-build absence, historical probe logging in ASTContext/SearchPathOptions/ClangImporter/Frontend/ModuleInterfaceLoader/SerializedModuleLoader, and cleanup of the TargetMachine workaround into a reviewable patch.

mainline_probe_new_driver_deps: 2026-07-31 new-driver probe found repos/swift-driver and repos/swiftpm on the true device but no corresponding targets in the existing compiler build graph. A separate swift-driver CMake probe configured with experimental OHOS swiftc, then build-swift-driver-ohos-probe2-20260731-012633.log failed on missing TSCBasic. Because the true device lacks git/curl, pinned dependencies were fetched on Windows and transferred to device _deps: swift-argument-parser 8f4d2753f0e4778c76d5f05ad16c74f707390531, swift-llbuild a3b8d34be319f1b3ba8de53c434386419dec1f4f, swift-tools-support-core 3b13e439a341bbbfe0f710c7d1be37221745ef1a, Yams 0d9ee7ea8c4ebd4a489ad7a73d5c6cad55d6fed3, swift-system 025bcb1165deab2e20d4eaba79967ce73013f496. Configure then exposed and passed several layers: use real clang-15 plus CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ARG1=--driver-mode=g++ instead of the HMDFS clang++ shell wrapper, stage sqlite3 3.49.1 for llbuild, and build SwiftSystem before TSC. SwiftSystem required an OHOS O_ACCMODE shim because OHOS fcntl.h defines O_ACCMODE through O_SEARCH, which ClangImporter cannot import; O_PATH was verified importable and the experiment uses 03 | O_PATH. SwiftSystem then hit signal 11 in SystemPackage direct object emission even with CMAKE_Swift_COMPILATION_MODE=wholemodule and CMAKE_Swift_NUM_THREADS=1. build-install-swift-system-ohos-wmo-20260731-015452.log crashes on swiftc -frontend -c -primary-file Sources/System/Errno.swift, while the same frontend command changed to -emit-ir succeeds in probe-swiftsystem-errno-emit-ir-20260731-015452.log with OUT_SIZE=292096. Next likely path is SwiftSystem IR-to-OHOS-clang object handoff, then install SwiftSystem and resume TSC/LLBuild/SwiftDriver.
mainline_probe_foundationcollections_handoff: 2026-07-31 Foundation probe configured successfully after staging SwiftSystem and libdispatch, using XML and Networking disabled for the first core-module pass: configure-foundation-ohos-probe1-20260731-033718.log CONFIG_RC=0. A direct ninja Foundation build then failed at _FoundationCollections direct object emission with signal 11 in build-foundation-ohos-probe1-j1-20260731-033842.log. A driver-level WMO emit-ir probe returned rc 254/signal 5 but wrote a non-empty IR file, so the experiment moved to primary-file IR handoff. The handoff emits the module once, then for each primary source runs swiftc -frontend -emit-ir -primary-file, opt -verify -disable-output, opt -S -strip-debug, and /bin/sh toolchain/ohos-clang -target aarch64-unknown-linux-ohos -fPIC -c. Bounded probes passed for the first 5, 20, and 60 primary files: probe-foundationcollections-primary-handoff-20260731-040138.log END=2026-07-31 04:05:12 CST rc=0, probe-foundationcollections-primary-handoff-20260731-040621.log END=2026-07-31 04:13:24 CST rc=0, and probe-foundationcollections-primary-handoff-20260731-041457.log END=2026-07-31 04:32:28 CST rc=0. The full 155-primary handoff then passed in probe-foundationcollections-primary-handoff-20260731-133956.log with END=2026-07-31 14:22:54 CST rc=0. audit_foundation_handoff.py reported TOTAL=155 EXISTS_NONZERO=155 MODULE_OK=1 MISSING=0 ZERO=0, and archive-foundationcollections-irhandoff-20260731-143723.log produced lib/lib_FoundationCollections.a size 4267042. The foundation build tree was temporarily patched only for Swift_COMPILER___FoundationCollections_unscanned_Release and Swift_STATIC_LIBRARY_LINKER___FoundationCollections_Release so Ninja recorded the audited handoff outputs without rerunning direct swiftc -c; backup rules.ninja.pre-foundationcollections-irhandoff-20260731-143654 was kept. ninja-foundationcollections-rulespatched-20260731-143723.log returned rc=0 and ninja -n _FoundationCollections reported no work. Resuming Foundation in build-foundation-after-foundationcollections-j1-20260731-143809.log advanced through _FoundationCShims and FoundationICU C/C++ compilation, then failed in FoundationEssentials semantic analysis: secure_getenv is missing, FTS/FTSENT/fts_* are missing, Regex/RegexComponent are absent, and macro declarations are unsupported. probe-foundation-frontend-features-20260731-145819.log confirmed REGEX_RC=1, MACRO_RC=1, missing implicit _Concurrency, and missing implicit _StringProcessing. The current next mainline work is enabling/staging Swift stdlib feature modules _Concurrency, _RegexParser, _StringProcessing, and RegexBuilder for OHOS, including SWIFT_MODULE_DEPENDS_OHOS Glibc mappings and a source path for the external string-processing library, before retrying FoundationEssentials and the SwiftPM/driver path.
mainline_probe_stdlib_feature_modules: 2026-07-31 continued the mainline Swift route by staging the official Swift 6.0.3 string-processing source repository swiftlang/swift-experimental-string-processing tag swift-6.0.3-RELEASE commit 9c50f6da9be4b4a828cec31adbef758a6c3673d6. The transferred archive was verified with sha256 19b615d31d904326bbb73fed9510524c66db3a00a7b133bc0fa2f681b48023b4 and unpacked on the true device at /storage/Users/currentUser/swift-mainline-ohos-src/deps/swift-experimental-string-processing-src. The existing OHOS Swift build tree was reconfigured with SWIFT_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_CONCURRENCY=ON, SWIFT_CONCURRENCY_GLOBAL_EXECUTOR=hooked, SWIFT_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_STRING_PROCESSING=ON, and SWIFT_PATH_TO_STRING_PROCESSING_SOURCE pointing to that staged source. reconfigure-swift-stdlib-concurrency-stringprocessing-20260731-161744.log completed with CMAKE_RC=0 and generated OHOS targets for _Concurrency, _RegexParser, _StringProcessing, and RegexBuilder. The first guarded build is nice -n 10 ninja -j1 swift_Concurrency-ohos in build-swift-Concurrency-ohos-j1-20260731-161949.log. Initial monitoring shows a live single-job Swift compiler C++ incremental rebuild caused by the experimental stdlib switches; no new source failure has been observed yet.

connection_boundary:   2026-07-30 14:56 CST: original Swift build host 10.62.230.12 was pingable but refused direct SSH on :2222 and old-O SakuraFrp on frp-tip.com:56267 through repeated checks. frp-tip.com:33332 was reachable but lacks /storage/Users/currentUser/swift-mainline-ohos-src, so it cannot resume the current incremental build without migration. No new Swift source failure was observed in this disconnected window.

forbid_1_pattern:       requires\\(\"swift/.*\"\\)|tool_requires\\(\"swift/.*\"\\)
forbid_1_reason:        No Swift toolchain recipe is currently published in ohpcd; declaring a nonexistent Conan dependency moves the failure to dependency resolution and hides the platform boundary.
forbid_1_consequence:   The package fails W0/L0i instead of preserving the real Swift toolchain migration evidence.
forbid_2_pattern:       copy.*linux.*swift.*bin|install.*swift.*without.*sign|skip.*swift.*version
forbid_2_reason:        Unsigned Linux/glibc ELF binaries do not prove the Swift toolchain is executable on OHOS, and skipping version checks can produce an unusable package.
forbid_2_consequence:   A PR may pass static checks while all SwiftPM builds still fail at runtime.
forbid_3_pattern:       bundle.*glibc.*without.*policy|create.*/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1
forbid_3_reason:        Adding a glibc loader/runtime compatibility layer is a separate toolchain/platform decision and must not be hidden inside an unrelated library PR.
forbid_3_consequence:   The package may violate system policy, collide with runtime libraries, or become non-reproducible.

